  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Clear as a minimal envelope and good on sparse, authority-free dispatch, but this scenario is only indirectly covered because reachability semantics, local receipts, and proof that Bob kept the forwarding promise are all delegated to payload and wrapper protocols.",
    "strengths": [
      "Deterministic CBOR canonical bytes give stable local evidence objects.",
      "Unknown-pCID store/forward behavior supports sparse knowledge without a central routing or schema authority.",
      "Strict pCID dispatch and wrapper-based signatures create very clear layer boundaries."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The specimen does not define a route-specific object or record shape for the reachability promise.",
      "It has no built-in receipt or hop-evidence convention for showing Bob kept the promise.",
      "Opaque payloads and wrappers reduce human and LLM auditability when handler specs are missing."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Different wrapper conventions may fragment interoperable routing evidence.",
      "If pCID-linked specs are not durably recoverable, future auditors may retain bytes but lose meaning.",
      "Its draft, unfrozen status raises migration-churn risk before stable routing conventions emerge."
    ],
